content.text:
**Found the root cause!**

Both CGAL and SH compute the **same intersection** (area = 69300000), but with **different vertex orderings**:

**CGAL (simplify_old):**
```
(-7000, 10000)  ← first vertex (top-left)
(-7000, 100)
(0, 100)
(0, 10000)
```

**SH (simplify_SH):**
```
(-7000, 100)  ← first vertex (bottom-left)
(0, 100)
(0, 10000)
(-7000, 10000)
```

Both are CCW and represent the same shape, but the algorithm takes `*outer_boundary().vertices_begin()` as the "representative point" of the intersection. Since the first vertex differs, the simplified trajectory diverges.

## The Fix

Instead of taking the first vertex, find a **consistent representative** - e.g., the vertex with minimum x (and if tied, minimum y):

```cpp
// Instead of:
buffer[1] = *new_S[i].begin()->outer_boundary().vertices_begin();

// Use:
const auto& outer = new_S[i].begin()->outer_boundary();
Point rep = *outer.vertices_begin();
for (auto it = outer.vertices_begin(); it != outer.vertices_end(); ++it) {
    if (CGAL::to_double(it->x()) < CGAL::to_double(rep.x()) ||
        (CGAL::to_double(it->x()) == CGAL::to_double(rep.x()) && 
         CGAL::to_double(it->y()) < CGAL::to_double(rep.y()))) {
        rep = *it;
    }
}
buffer[1] = rep;
```

Want me to implement this fix?

